{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21ec7e39d99b57bc5ab9", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Mechanical Properties of Fluids - Test 2", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 50, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2b7"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b270", "title": "Physics Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b1f6", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e40fd5f1ef260f84ed1e97"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e3d"], "question": {"text": "<p>A light cylindrical vessel is kept on a horizontal surface. Area of the base is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>.</mo></math> A hole of cross-sectional area <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi></math>&nbsp;is made just at its bottom side. The minimum coefficient of friction necessary to prevent sliding the vessel due to the impact force of the emerging liquid is</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fead106dd-1a60-4caf-918b-57c284ac3732-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 187px; height: 171px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "f8f458", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b1f7"}, {"id": "4568a9", "text": "None of these", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b1f8"}, {"id": "3fb555", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow><mi>A</mi></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b1f9"}, {"id": "df0d06", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mi>A</mi></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b1fa"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b1fb", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4782"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87c8"], "question": {"text": "Correct Bernoulli's equation is (symbols have their usual meaning) :"}, "options": [{"id": "78f332", "text": "$P+m g h+\\frac{1}{2} m v^2=$ constant", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b1fc"}, {"id": "2fcc90", "text": "$P+\\rho g h+\\frac{1}{2} \\rho v^2=$ constant", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b1fd"}, {"id": "38313f", "text": "$P+\\rho g h+\\rho v^2=$ constant", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b1fe"}, {"id": "b475b1", "text": "$P+\\frac{1}{2} \\rho g h+\\frac{1}{2} \\rho v^2=$ constant", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b1ff"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b200", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87c7"], "question": {"text": "Two liquids A and B have $\\theta_{\\mathrm{A}}$ and $\\theta_{\\mathrm{B}}$ as contact angles in a capillary tube. If $K=\\cos \\theta_A / \\cos \\theta_B$. then identify the correct statement:"}, "options": [{"id": "a6a2a5", "text": "$K$ is negative, then liquid $A$ and liquid $B$ have convex meniscus.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b201"}, {"id": "ceff6c", "text": "$K$ is negative, then liquid $A$ and liquid $B$ have concave meniscus.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b202"}, {"id": "29662f", "text": "K is negative, then liquid A has concave meniscus and liquid $B$ has convex meniscus", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b203"}, {"id": "ed510a", "text": "K is zero, then liquid A has convex meniscus and liquid B has concave meniscus.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b204"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b205", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb9e66f9ce3f243b52e9"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52bc"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements: one is labelled as</p><p><strong>Assertion A</strong> and the other is labelled as <strong>Reason R</strong></p><p><strong>Assertion A</strong> : A spherical body of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#177;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> having a particular density is falling through a liquid of constant density. The percentage error in the calculation of its terminal velocity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>%</mo></math>.</p><p><strong>Reason R</strong> : The terminal velocity of the spherical body falling through the liquid is inversely proportional to its radius. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "493a7f", "text": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b206"}, {"id": "6f12dd", "text": "Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b207"}, {"id": "f863af", "text": "A is true but R is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b208"}, {"id": "8a3d21", "text": "A is false but R is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b209"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b20a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f37"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4ef9"], "question": {"text": "A wooden block floating in a bucket of water has <math><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac></math> of its volume submerged. When certain amount of an oil is poured into the bucket, it is found that the block is just under the oil surface with half of its volume under water and half in oil. The density of oil relative to that of water is:"}, "options": [{"id": "c3b93d", "text": "<math><mn>0.8</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b20b"}, {"id": "43768e", "text": "<math><mn>0.7</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b20c"}, {"id": "aabe70", "text": "<math><mn>0.5</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b20d"}, {"id": "61915d", "text": "<math><mn>0.6</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b20e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b20f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e567de499d1e49588f9cd5"], "concepts": ["60e567fa499d1e49588fa01c"], "question": {"text": "Two cylindrical vessels of equal cross sectional area of $2 \\mathrm{~m}^2$ contain water up to height 10 m and 6 m , respectively. If the vessels are connected at their bottom then the work done by the force of gravity is : (Density of water is $10^3 \\mathrm{~kg} / \\mathrm{m}^3$ and $\\mathrm{g}=10 \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}^2$)"}, "options": [{"id": "b29be9", "text": "$1 \\times 10^5 \\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b210"}, {"id": "771564", "text": "$4 \\times 10^4 \\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b211"}, {"id": "fdf73e", "text": "$6 \\times 10^4 \\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b212"}, {"id": "62d0cc", "text": "$8 \\times 10^4 \\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b213"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b214", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4782"], "concepts": ["6162fafa6ed13040ec8499ce"], "question": {"text": "An ideal fluid flows (laminar flow) through a pipe of non-uniform diameter. The maximum and minimum diameters of the pipes are <math><mn>6.4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"></mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> and <math><mn>4.8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"></mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> , respectively. The ratio of the minimum and the maximum velocities of fluid in this pipe is:"}, "options": [{"id": "0e368f", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b215"}, {"id": "5e0bb9", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b216"}, {"id": "cdafdb", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b217"}, {"id": "9acab3", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>81</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>256</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b218"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b219", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8ed2"], "question": {"text": "<p>A water drop of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> is broken into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>729</mn></math> equal droplets. If surface tension of water is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>75</mn><mi>dyne</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>cm</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, then the gain in surface energy upto first decimal place will be</p><p>[Given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn></math>]</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "a6d9d2", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b21a"}, {"id": "7b629a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b21b"}, {"id": "1cb6ef", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b21c"}, {"id": "61c8a3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b21d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b21e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e40fd5f1ef260f84ed1e97"], "concepts": ["6162faf76ed13040ec8497c3"], "question": {"text": "The pressure acting on a submarine is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Pa</mi></math> at a certain depth. If the depth is doubled, the percentage increase in the pressure acting on the submarine would be: (Assume that atmospheric pressure is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Pa</mi></math>&nbsp;density of water is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>ms</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>)"}, "options": [{"id": "f41126", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>200</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>%</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b21f"}, {"id": "62d786", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>200</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo>%</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b220"}, {"id": "f3c8fc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>200</mn></mfrac><mo>%</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b221"}, {"id": "c712d8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>200</mn></mfrac><mo>%</mo></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b222"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b223", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f37"], "concepts": ["60c9e848ea076dc381c0df47"], "question": {"text": "A cubical block of side <math><mn>0.5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> floats on water with <math><mn>30</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></math> of its volume under water. What is the maximum weight that can be put on the block without fully submerging it under water?<br /><br>[Take, density of water <math><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></math> ]"}, "options": [{"id": "9bfc56", "text": "<math><mn>87.5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b224"}, {"id": "d21888", "text": "<math><mn>65.4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b225"}, {"id": "a1f41b", "text": "<math><mn>30.1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b226"}, {"id": "6f6429", "text": "<math><mn>46.3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b227"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b228", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb9e66f9ce3f243b52e9"], "concepts": ["60e567fa499d1e49588fa01e"], "question": {"text": "The velocity of a small ball of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;and density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>, when dropped in a container filled with glycerine, becomes constant after some time. If the density of glycerine is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>, then the viscous force acting on the ball, will be"}, "options": [{"id": "dc5fb0", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b229"}, {"id": "2761d5", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b22a"}, {"id": "bce495", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b22b"}, {"id": "d8f6f0", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b22c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b22d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5648"], "question": {"text": "Pressure inside a soap bubble is greater than the pressure outside by an amount :\n(given : $\\mathrm{R}=$ Radius of bubble $\\mathrm{S}=$ Surface tension of bubble)"}, "options": [{"id": "fb8abb", "text": "$\\frac{2 S}{R}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b22e"}, {"id": "0fb5e3", "text": "$\\frac{4 R}{S}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b22f"}, {"id": "1c312f", "text": "$\\frac{S}{R}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b230"}, {"id": "1767b3", "text": "$\\frac{4 \\mathrm{~S}}{\\mathrm{R}}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b231"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b232", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4782"], "concepts": ["61ada03cdedd066c316e55c8"], "question": {"text": "Water flows m a horizontal tube (see figure). The pressure of water changes by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>700</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>N</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math> between <math><mi>A</mi></math> and <math><mi>B</mi></math> where the area of cross section are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></math> respectively. Find the rate of flow of water through the tube. (density of water <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math> )<br /><br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F395a7730-07a2-4438-a9b9-7122560771ee.jpeg?alt=media\" />"}, "options": [{"id": "cf65af", "text": "<math><mn>3020</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"></mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></math>  <br/><em>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b233"}, {"id": "1