><msub><mi>&#961;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b245"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b246", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b51"], "question": {"text": "A capillary tube of radius 0.1 mm is partly dipped in water (surface tension $70 \\mathrm{dyn} / \\mathrm{cm}$ and glass water contact angle $\\simeq 0^{\\circ}$) with $30^{\\circ}$ inclined with vertical. The length of water risen in the capillary is ________ cm.<br/>$\\left(\\right.$ Take $\\left.\\mathrm{g}=9.8 \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}^2\\right)$"}, "options": [{"id": "47d8b8", "text": "$\\frac{82}{5}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b247"}, {"id": "52f5c2", "text": "$\\frac{57}{2}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b248"}, {"id": "19860e", "text": "$\\frac{71}{5}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b249"}, {"id": "b9b29b", "text": "$\\frac{68}{5}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b24a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b24b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4782"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4738"], "question": {"text": "Water from a tap emerges vertically downwards with an initial speed of <math><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>1.0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> . The cross <math><mo>-</mo></math> sectional area of the tap is <math><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math> . Assume that the pressure is constant throughout the stream of water and that the flow is streamlined. The cross <math><mo>-</mo></math> sectional area of the stream, <math><mn>0.15</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> below the tap would be:<br />(Take <math><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math> )"}, "options": [{"id": "e28e9a", "text": "<math><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b24c"}, {"id": "1a2e43", "text": "<math><mn>5</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b24d"}, {"id": "2c27eb", "text": "<math><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b24e"}, {"id": "c68dd3", "text": "<math><mn>5</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b24f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b250", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f37"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4ef9"], "question": {"text": "A sphere of relative density $\\sigma$ and diameter $D$ has concentric cavity of diameter $d$. The ratio of $\\frac{D}{d}$, if it just floats on water in a tank is :"}, "options": [{"id": "079cf7", "text": "$\\left(\\frac{\\sigma-2}{\\sigma+2}\\right)^{1 / 3}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b251"}, {"id": "c927f7", "text": "$\\left(\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sigma-1}\\right)^{1 / 3}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b252"}, {"id": "8b4634", "text": "$\\left(\\frac{\\sigma-1}{\\sigma}\\right)^{1 / 3}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b253"}, {"id": "e351c8", "text": "$\\left(\\frac{\\sigma+1}{\\sigma-1}\\right)^{1 / 3}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b254"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b255", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb9e66f9ce3f243b52e9"], "concepts": ["60e567fa499d1e49588fa01e"], "question": {"text": "<p>Given below are two statements :</p><p>Statement (I) : Viscosity of gases is greater than that of liquids.</p><p>Statement (II) : Surface tension of a liquid decreases due to the presence of insoluble impurities.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "b424f5", "text": "Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b256"}, {"id": "f2e932", "text": "Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b257"}, {"id": "b1f8be", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b258"}, {"id": "26d849", "text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b259"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:29:02.851Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:29:02.851Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2b5"}, {"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2b3", "title": "Physics Numerical", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b296", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4782"], "concepts": ["6162fafa6ed13040ec8499ce"], "question": {"text": "<p>Figure below shows a liquid being pushed out of the tube by a piston having area of cross section <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>cm</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. The area of cross section at the outlet is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>mm</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. If the piston is pushed at a speed of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, the speed of outgoing fluid is _____&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F58837c77-e514-42ac-9056-d04f4e6801e7.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 96px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "23add9", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b297"}, {"id": "0e59d3", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b298"}, {"id": "8f797a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b299"}, {"id": "584c14", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b29a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b29b", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b65"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b51"], "question": {"text": "When a long glass capillary tube of radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>015</mn><mi>cm</mi></math> is dipped in a liquid, the liquid rises to a height of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> within it. If the contact angle between the liquid and glass to close to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, the surface tension of the liquid, in milliNewton&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#961;</mi><mtext>(liqued)&#160;</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>900</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math> (Given answer in closed integer)"}, "options": [{"id": "a35f79", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b29c"}, {"id": "cee608", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b29d"}, {"id": "7dcb1c", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b29e"}, {"id": "14ccbc", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b29f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2a0", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e567de499d1e49588f9cd5"], "concepts": ["6117b8bd67dbe2603c3e8596"], "question": {"text": "A tube of length 1 m is filled completely with an ideal liquid of mass 2 M , and closed at both ends. The tube is rotated uniformly in horizontal plane about one of its ends. If the force exerted by the liquid at the other end is F then angular velocity of the tube is $\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mathrm{F}}{\\alpha \\mathrm{M}}}$ in SI unit. The value of $\\alpha$ is __________."}, "options": [{"id": "a29f30", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2a1"}, {"id": "6f6047", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2a2"}, {"id": "0b28cd", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2a3"}, {"id": "79a453", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2a4"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2a5", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f37"], "concepts": ["60c9e848ea076dc381c0df47"], "question": {"text": "<p>The velocity of a small ball of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math>&nbsp;and density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>cc</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> when dropped in a container filled with glycerine becomes constant after some time. If the density of glycerine is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>cc</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, then the value of viscous force acting on the ball will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>, the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is _____ .</p><p>[use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>]</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "376640", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2a6"}, {"id": "b3c939", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2a7"}, {"id": "28177c", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2a8"}, {"id": "4eaf8a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2a9"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2aa", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfab"], "topics": ["6117b89167dbe2603c3e818e"], "concepts": ["6162fafb6ed13040ec849a85"], "question": {"text": "The water is filled up to a height of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> in a tank having vertical sidewalls. A hole is made in one of the walls at a depth&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math> below the water level. The value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math> for which the emerging stream of water strikes the ground at the maximum range is _____&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>."}, "options": [{"id": "02625f", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2ab"}, {"id": "b9e1f3", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2ac"}, {"id": "1e589a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2ad"}, {"id": "05b75a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f2e2ee0e063d924b2ae"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283c623cf4b3ad8f125e5", "title": "JEE Numerical", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, 